 ("A case-control study samples 100 cases and 100 controls. Can the study directly estimate population incidence?","No; the investigator selected the number of cases and controls rather than following a population at risk.",["Yes, by dividing controls by cases.","Yes, because every case-control study estimates person-time.","Only if the odds ratio equals prevalence."],"Case-control sampling does not supply the population denominator needed for incidence."),
 ("There are 12 new cases among 300 people at risk over six months. What must be stated with cumulative incidence?","The follow-up period and the population at risk at its start.",["Only the number of existing cases after follow-up.","The mean age without a denominator.","The diagnostic test's negative predictive value."],"Risk is meaningful when the time interval and at-risk population are clear."),
 ("A screening program detects more cases, but the incidence of severe disease stays unchanged. What could explain this?","Earlier diagnosis or overdiagnosis may increase detected prevalence without changing disease occurrence.",["Incidence and detection are always identical.","Every detected case proves prevention failed.","The population denominator is irrelevant."],"More intensive detection can find existing or indolent disease without reducing severe outcomes."),
