 ("A trial uses concealed allocation before participants enter either group. What problem does this mainly reduce?","Predictable assignment and selection bias at enrollment.",["Outcome misclassification after treatment.","Confounding from age in all analyses.","Loss to follow-up."],"Concealment prevents recruiters from anticipating the next assignment."),
 ("A cohort loses 40% of participants, mostly those with severe symptoms. What is the concern?","Attrition bias if loss relates to exposure and outcome.",["The cohort becomes randomized.","The population gains a valid denominator automatically.","The outcome is necessarily misclassified."],"Differential loss can make observed follow-up unrepresentative."),
 ("An intervention's measured outcome is defined differently in treatment and comparison groups. What risk follows?","Measurement or information bias.",["Confounding is impossible.","Randomization has been retroactively undone.","The denominator must be person-time."],"Inconsistent measurement can create a systematic difference unrelated to a true effect."),
 ("A study measures a mediator after exposure and before outcome. Why can adjusting for it be problematic?","It may block part of the effect the analysis intends to estimate.",["It always removes all bias.","It changes an observational study into a trial.","It increases the sample size."],"Adjustment for a causal intermediate can remove mediated effects or introduce bias."),
 ("A cohort finds risk ratio 1.8, but the confidence interval includes 1.0. What is the appropriate interpretation?","The estimate suggests higher risk, but the data are compatible with no association at the stated confidence level.",["The exposure is proven harmful.","The risk ratio is invalid because it exceeds 1.","The association is certainly protective."],"An interval including the null value signals statistical uncertainty, not proof of no effect."),
 ("Why does randomization help estimate an intervention effect?","It tends to balance measured and unmeasured baseline factors across groups.",["It guarantees blinding and perfect adherence.","It eliminates random error.","It prevents all participants from withdrawing."],"Random assignment reduces systematic baseline differences on average."),
 ("An analyst compares people who completed a program with those who did not, although completion was voluntary. What caution is needed?","Completion may reflect motivation or health, so the groups may not be comparable.",["The comparison is automatically causal.","Voluntary participation removes confounding.","Only sample size matters."],"Post-enrollment selection can create substantial differences unrelated to program effect."),
 ("A case-control study matches cases and controls by age. What should investigators avoid?","Ignoring the matched design when analyzing the data.",["Measuring exposure.","Defining cases.","Reporting the matching criteria."],"Analysis should account for the matching to respect the sampling structure."),
 ("A repeated cross-sectional survey samples a new group each year. What can it show well?","Population-level trends in prevalence over time.",["Individual disease progression for each participant.","The exact sequence of exposure and outcome for each person.","A treatment effect without a comparison."],"Repeated independent samples track population patterns, not within-person changes."),
 ("A study has strong association, dose-response, and temporality but is observational. What is the best stance?","Consider these as evidence supporting causality while evaluating alternative explanations and study limitations.",["Association alone proves causality.","Observational evidence can never inform causation.","A dose-response pattern eliminates all bias."],"Causal reasoning integrates multiple evidence features and possible biases."),
