 ("A workplace provides gloves, but the chemical can permeate the glove material. What should be done?","Select protective material suitable for the chemical and exposure duration, while prioritizing source controls.",["Assume any glove blocks every chemical.","Double gloves without checking compatibility.","Remove training and signage."],"PPE must be matched to the hazard; unsuitable materials can fail."),
 ("An occupational disease cluster is reported. Why compare affected and unaffected workers?","To identify differences in tasks or exposures that may explain the pattern.",["To prove a cause from job title alone.","To avoid collecting exposure information.","Because unaffected workers cannot provide useful data."],"Comparison groups help test whether exposure patterns differ by health status."),
 ("A city monitors particulate matter near schools. What additional information helps interpret the data?","Time, location, meteorology, monitoring method, and relevant population exposure.",["Only the school names.","Whether students like science.","The number of clinic rooms."],"Context determines whether readings reflect exposure and how they relate to health outcomes."),
 ("A workplace training course is delivered, but workers lack authority to stop unsafe work. What remains weak?","The organizational controls and ability to act on hazard information.",["The color of safety equipment.","The incidence denominator.","The laboratory assay's specificity."],"Training alone cannot compensate for unsafe systems or lack of control over hazards."),
 ("A city replaces lead service lines and offers blood lead testing. How do the actions differ?","Line replacement controls the source; testing assesses exposure or its effects.",["Both are only tertiary prevention.","Testing removes lead from water.","Line replacement diagnoses individual exposure."],"Environmental control prevents exposure; biomonitoring helps identify affected people."),
 ("A chemical hazard has no feasible substitute and enclosure is possible. Which measure is preferred over relying only on masks?","Enclose the process and use ventilation to control emissions.",["Wait for illness.","Tell workers to hold their breath.","Remove all exposure monitoring."],"Engineering controls reduce hazard release at the source or along the exposure pathway."),
